\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Induced Euclidean volume on a subspace}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{H} be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, in
particular a subspace of #{(X_V,h_V)} with its induced topology and
inner product, and put #{k=\dim H}. Its
\newvocab{induced Euclidean measure} is the unique Borel measure
#{\lambda_H} such that every orthonormal chart
#{C:H\to\mathbb R^k} satisfies
##{C_*\lambda_H=\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}.}
Here an orthonormal chart is a linear isometric isomorphism, and the
target has \vocabk{coordinate Lebesgue measure}{fdip-002H} on its uncompleted
Borel sigma-algebra. For #{H=\{0\}}, #{\lambda_H} is unit mass at zero.
On an affine translate #{a+H}, use #{(\tau_a)_*\lambda_H}, where
#{\tau_a(h)=a+h}.}
\p{The characterization and its independence of the affine origin are
\ref{fdip-00JM}. They use \ref{fdip-002L}
and the translation convention of
\citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.}
